1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,398
Press the spring in a
pen, and it pushes back.

2
00:00:03,398 --> 00:00:06,209
Sit on a sofa, and its
springs push you up.

3
00:00:06,609 --> 00:00:08,601
A squeezed spring can do work.

4
00:00:08,601 --> 00:00:10,545
So it must be storing energy.

5
00:00:10,995 --> 00:00:12,862
Aarav loads his toy spring gun.

6
00:00:12,862 --> 00:00:15,888
He pushes the ball in, and
the spring gets shorter.

7
00:00:16,238 --> 00:00:19,732
He pulls the trigger, and the
ball shoots across the room.

8
00:00:19,732 --> 00:00:22,949
Where was that energy hiding,
and how much was there?

9
00:00:23,449 --> 00:00:26,936
By the end of this video, you
will work it out in joules.

10
00:00:27,586 --> 00:00:30,283
So what is the potential
energy of a spring?

11
00:00:30,633 --> 00:00:35,191
Stretch or squeeze a spring, and it
pulls or pushes back, towards its normal

12
00:00:35,191 --> 00:00:36,091
length.

13
00:00:36,206 --> 00:00:39,136
The further you stretch
it, the harder it pulls.

14
00:00:39,136 --> 00:00:41,328
Twice the stretch, twice the force.

15
00:00:41,728 --> 00:00:43,173
This is Hooke's law.

16
00:00:43,173 --> 00:00:44,680
F equals minus k x.

17
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:47,933
k is the spring constant,
in newtons per metre.

18
00:00:48,333 --> 00:00:51,522
The work you do to stretch
it is stored in the spring.

19
00:00:51,522 --> 00:00:53,260
That is its potential energy.

20
00:00:53,910 --> 00:00:54,842
Let's run it.

21
00:00:54,842 --> 00:00:58,525
An eight kilogram block on a
smooth floor, tied to a spring.

22
00:00:58,525 --> 00:01:00,715
k is two hundred newtons per metre.

23
00:01:01,065 --> 00:01:02,833
Pull it ten centimetres to the right.

24
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:04,776
The spring pulls back with twenty newtons.

25
00:01:05,176 --> 00:01:06,616
Pull to twenty centimetres.

26
00:01:06,616 --> 00:01:08,588
Now it pulls back with forty newtons.

27
00:01:08,588 --> 00:01:09,488
Double.

28
00:01:09,504 --> 00:01:12,693
On the graph, the force
grows in a straight line.

29
00:01:12,693 --> 00:01:15,497
The work is the area under it, a triangle.

30
00:01:15,897 --> 00:01:20,235
At twenty centimetres, the area is
half, times zero point two, times forty.

31
00:01:20,235 --> 00:01:21,413
Four joules stored.

32
00:01:21,913 --> 00:01:22,813
Now let go.

33
00:01:22,753 --> 00:01:26,628
The spring pulls the block back,
and the stored energy becomes speed.

34
00:01:27,028 --> 00:01:29,609
Slow-mo replay, as it passes the middle.

35
00:01:29,959 --> 00:01:33,136
In the middle, the spring
is at its normal length.

36
00:01:33,136 --> 00:01:36,985
The force is zero, U is zero,
and the speed is the largest.

37
00:01:37,635 --> 00:01:39,821
Now the formula, step by step.

38
00:01:40,171 --> 00:01:41,071
Step one.

39
00:01:41,010 --> 00:01:44,744
To stretch it slowly, your
pull must match the spring.

40
00:01:44,744 --> 00:01:45,910
F equals k x.

41
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:47,560
Step two.

42
00:01:47,471 --> 00:01:49,825
The force grows from zero to k x.

43
00:01:49,825 --> 00:01:52,772
So we cannot just multiply
force by distance.

44
00:01:53,522 --> 00:01:54,422
Step three.

45
00:01:54,370 --> 00:01:57,077
The work is the area
under the force graph.

46
00:01:57,077 --> 00:01:59,693
A triangle, with base x and height k x.

47
00:02:00,443 --> 00:02:01,366
Step four.

48
00:02:01,366 --> 00:02:04,378
The area is half, times x, times k x.

49
00:02:04,378 --> 00:02:05,799
Half k x squared.

50
00:02:06,549 --> 00:02:07,449
Step five.

51
00:02:07,361 --> 00:02:08,751
That work is stored.

52
00:02:08,751 --> 00:02:12,790
U equals half k x squared, whether
you stretch it or squeeze it.

53
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:14,570
Remember this.

54
00:02:14,570 --> 00:02:18,392
A spring stretched or squeezed
by x stores half k x squared.

55
00:02:19,092 --> 00:02:21,324
Why x squared, and not just x?

56
00:02:21,324 --> 00:02:22,583
Follow the links.

57
00:02:22,933 --> 00:02:23,833
One.

58
00:02:23,401 --> 00:02:26,366
Double the stretch, and
the final force doubles.

59
00:02:26,766 --> 00:02:27,666
Two.

60
00:02:27,236 --> 00:02:29,413
The distance you pull also doubles.

61
00:02:29,813 --> 00:02:30,713
Three.

62
00:02:30,405 --> 00:02:33,760
So the triangle is twice
as wide and twice as tall.

63
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:35,806
Its area is four times bigger.

64
00:02:36,206 --> 00:02:37,106
Four.

65
00:02:36,752 --> 00:02:39,551
Double the stretch, four times the energy.

66
00:02:39,551 --> 00:02:41,199
Triple it, nine times.

67
00:02:41,799 --> 00:02:43,221
Now, side by side.

68
00:02:43,571 --> 00:02:45,908
Stretch the spring by ten centimetres.

69
00:02:45,908 --> 00:02:49,688
U is half, times two hundred,
times zero point one squared.

70
00:02:49,688 --> 00:02:50,588
One joule.

71
00:02:50,898 --> 00:02:52,834
Squeeze it by ten centimetres.

72
00:02:52,834 --> 00:02:56,394
x is minus zero point one,
but its square is the same.

73
00:02:56,394 --> 00:02:57,555
One joule again.

74
00:02:58,155 --> 00:02:59,196
Now you try.

75
00:02:59,196 --> 00:03:01,904
Spring A is stiff, k is four hundred.

76
00:03:01,904 --> 00:03:04,510
Spring B is soft, k is one hundred.

77
00:03:04,510 --> 00:03:05,915
Same pull on both.

78
00:03:05,915 --> 00:03:07,673
Which stores more energy?

79
00:03:07,673 --> 00:03:08,966
Pause and decide.

80
00:03:12,166 --> 00:03:13,241
The soft one!

81
00:03:13,241 --> 00:03:16,775
With the same force, it
stretches four times as far.

82
00:03:16,775 --> 00:03:18,737
U is F squared over two k.

83
00:03:19,386 --> 00:03:20,286
One more.

84
00:03:20,135 --> 00:03:22,144
Is the spring force conservative?

85
00:03:22,144 --> 00:03:23,267
Pause and think.

86
00:03:26,467 --> 00:03:27,367
Yes!

87
00:03:26,903 --> 00:03:30,827
Stretch it and let it come back,
and the spring's total work is zero.

88
00:03:30,827 --> 00:03:33,076
That is why it has a potential energy.

89
00:03:33,726 --> 00:03:35,417
Now, one worked example.

90
00:03:35,767 --> 00:03:39,485
Aarav's gun spring has k equal to
four hundred newtons per metre.

91
00:03:39,485 --> 00:03:43,150
He squeezes it five centimetres,
and fires a twenty gram ball.

92
00:03:43,150 --> 00:03:44,666
How fast does it leave?

93
00:03:45,166 --> 00:03:46,243
First, units.

94
00:03:46,243 --> 00:03:50,243
Zero point zero five metres, and
zero point zero two kilograms.

95
00:03:50,993 --> 00:03:52,056
Stored energy.

96
00:03:52,056 --> 00:03:56,129
Half, times four hundred, times
zero point zero five squared.

97
00:03:56,129 --> 00:03:57,759
Zero point five joules.

98
00:03:58,509 --> 00:04:00,612
All of it becomes kinetic energy.

99
00:04:00,612 --> 00:04:05,247
Half, times zero point zero two, times
v squared, equals zero point five.

100
00:04:05,997 --> 00:04:07,788
So v squared is fifty.

101
00:04:07,788 --> 00:04:11,533
v is about seven point zero
seven metres per second.

102
00:04:12,183 --> 00:04:13,645
Now, the exam trap.

103
00:04:13,995 --> 00:04:18,069
Many students say the work to stretch
a spring is force times stretch,

104
00:04:18,069 --> 00:04:19,072
k x times x.

105
00:04:19,422 --> 00:04:21,574
But the force grows from zero.

106
00:04:21,574 --> 00:04:24,143
Use the triangle: half k x squared.

107
00:04:24,143 --> 00:04:26,142
And always put x in metres.

108
00:04:26,792 --> 00:04:28,031
Where is this asked?

109
00:04:28,031 --> 00:04:32,386
In spring numericals: a block hits a
spring, or the work between two stretches.

110
00:04:32,986 --> 00:04:33,967
Exam level now.

111
00:04:33,967 --> 00:04:37,906
A spring with k equal to eight hundred
newtons per metre is already stretched

112
00:04:37,906 --> 00:04:39,095
by five centimetres.

113
00:04:39,095 --> 00:04:42,477
How much more work is needed to
stretch it to ten centimetres?

114
00:04:42,477 --> 00:04:43,499
Pause and solve.

115
00:04:48,899 --> 00:04:50,315
The answer is A.

116
00:04:50,315 --> 00:04:51,434
Three joules.

117
00:04:51,934 --> 00:04:56,320
At ten centimetres, U is half, times
eight hundred, times zero point one

118
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:57,220
squared.

119
00:04:56,979 --> 00:04:57,879
Four joules.

120
00:04:58,378 --> 00:05:02,989
At five centimetres, U is half, times
eight hundred, times zero point zero

121
00:05:02,989 --> 00:05:03,963
five squared.

122
00:05:03,963 --> 00:05:04,863
One joule.

123
00:05:05,293 --> 00:05:07,764
The extra work is four minus one.

124
00:05:07,764 --> 00:05:08,850
Three joules.

125
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:10,990
Here is your recap.

126
00:05:10,990 --> 00:05:12,573
Screenshot this card.

127
00:05:15,773 --> 00:05:17,959
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

128
00:05:18,359 --> 00:05:19,259
One.

129
00:05:18,879 --> 00:05:22,885
Why is the energy half k x
squared, and not k x squared?

130
00:05:23,785 --> 00:05:24,685
Two.

131
00:05:24,233 --> 00:05:25,445
Double the stretch.

132
00:05:25,445 --> 00:05:27,475
What happens to the stored energy?

133
00:05:28,375 --> 00:05:29,275
Three.

134
00:05:28,914 --> 00:05:31,273
Does a squeezed spring store energy too?

135
00:05:32,373 --> 00:05:37,255
Next time: swing a bucket of water
over your head, and not a drop falls.

136
00:05:37,255 --> 00:05:40,205
Find out how in motion
in a vertical circle.
